systemctl show fixtures for parseShow (internal/providers/nexusd/system.go)

Each file is the output of

    systemctl show nexusd.service -p ActiveState -p SubState -p Result -p MainPID -p NRestarts

in one unit state:

    active.txt              Type=notify unit after READY=1
    activating.txt          started, READY=1 not yet sent
    failed-start-limit.txt  a binary that exits at start, after the start limit
                            (StartLimitBurst=5 in StartLimitIntervalSec=10s)
    auto-restart.txt        between a failed start and its Restart=on-failure re-spawn

Provenance: NOT captured from a running systemd. These files were written
by hand (WI 4.1) from the documented format, because the T1 harness capture
belongs to WI 7.2. Sources:

  - systemctl(1), https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemctl.html:
    `show` "is intended to be used whenever computer-parsable output is
    required"; `--value` "only print the value, and skip the property name
    and '='" (so each line is NAME=VALUE); `-p` "If specified more than
    once, all properties with the specified names are shown."
  - org.freedesktop.systemd1(5), https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/org.freedesktop.systemd1.html:
    `readonly u MainPID`, `readonly u NRestarts`, `readonly s Result`,
    `readonly s ActiveState` (active, inactive, failed, activating,
    deactivating, maintenance, reloading, refreshing), `readonly s SubState`.
  - systemd.exec(5), https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html,
    $SERVICE_RESULT: the Result values, including "exit-code" and
    "start-limit-hit".

The line order (service properties MainPID, Result, NRestarts before the
unit properties ActiveState, SubState) is the order systemctl prints bus
properties in, not the order of the -p flags; parseShow does not depend on
it. The PIDs and NRestarts values are illustrative.

systemd version: none (hand-written; see above). TODO for WI 7.2 / TC-SM-129:
replace each file with a capture from the T1 harness

    docker exec <t1-container> systemctl show nexusd.service \
        -p ActiveState -p SubState -p Result -p MainPID -p NRestarts

and record `docker exec <t1-container> systemctl --version | head -1` here.
